  "scenario": "A buyer has used a phone for years, finds indoor volleyball photos blurred or noisy, and has about 700 dollars for a first dedicated camera. The budget and phone symptoms are user anchors, not measured results. The frozen shopping packet contains an Olympus E-M1 body page at 668 dollars, a Panasonic ZS70 travel-compact bundle at 379.08 dollars, a Panasonic LX10 one-inch fixed-lens bundle at 514.87 dollars, a Canon 80D body page at 798 dollars, a Micro Four Thirds Panasonic 35-100mm f/2.8 lens page at 897.99 dollars, and a Canon-compatible Sigma 50-100mm f/1.8 lens page at 999 dollars. Frozen model pages identify the E-M1 as a 2013 Micro Four Thirds interchangeable-lens camera, the LX10 as a 2016 fixed-lens camera with a 13.2 by 8.8mm sensor and 24-72mm-equivalent f/1.4-f/2.8 lens, the 80D as a 2016 DSLR, and the ZS70 row as a 2017 1/2.3-type 30x compact with a 24-720mm-equivalent f/3.3-f/6.4 lens. General pages explain sensor format and angle of view, f-number and light gathering, exposure time, motion blur, image noise, autofocus, and sports-photography equipment. Three community pages contain only scoped author reports or questions about camera settings in dark capture and phone noise, focus, or oversharpening. No captured source supplies a same-gym controlled comparison, a verified current delivered offer or used-camera condition, a complete compatible system under 700 dollars, or a universal rule that sensor size or release year alone predicts indoor-sports keeper rate.",
